## Soft State Notes

When this turn's outcome makes a durable, non-plot-relevant change to
the world — cleared webs, a rearranged room, a marked door, a fact the
player discovered by examining something — record it in
`soft_state_notes` so future turns remember it.  These notes are
stored and shown back to you (and the ruling GM) in later briefings as
`room_notes` / `entity_notes`.

Each note is an object:

| Field | Type | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| `field` | string | `"room_note"` or `"entity_note"` |
| `entity_id` | string | (`entity_note` only) ID of an entity in the current room, or `"player"` for a note about the player that follows them across rooms |
| `new_value` | string | The note text — a single self-contained statement of what is now true |
| `reason` | string | Why this change occurred this turn |

Guidelines:

- Record only what actually happened per the EngineResult and your own
  narration — never speculate about outcomes the engine did not
  confirm.
- Notes are for durable world/state changes, not for plot-significant
  events (the engine tracks those) or trivia not worth remembering.
  Most turns need no notes; omit `soft_state_notes` or use `[]`.
- Write notes as self-contained statements, since they will be read
  out of context later (e.g. "The webs across the axe handle are
  partially cleared", not "The webs are cleared now").
- Do not contradict hard state: a note claiming a living entity is
  dead will be rejected by the engine, as will notes about entities
  not in the current room.
- After an NPC is introduced by name to the player, add a note about
  this; it's useful to track, as the narrator should avoid identifying
  NPCs by name until known to the player.
- For `ooc_discussion` turns, never record notes.

